<h2>Appendix · Theory map</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:20%">Framework</th><th style="width:31%">Mechanism and test</th><th style="width:22%">Holds only if</th><th>What we see</th></tr>
<tr><td>Katz &amp; Shapiro (1985): network externalities</td><td>Firms with a large installed base may prefer incompatibility. Test: do incumbents block a shared standard?</td><td>Incumbents gain more from lock-in than from a bigger market</td><td>Truckmakers own the charging network and reject a CATL-led format</td></tr>
<tr><td>David (1985); Arthur (1989): lock-in</td><td>Early adoption can tip a market to one technology. Test: is a format gaining irreversible share?</td><td>Increasing returns to adoption</td><td>EU infrastructure is tipping toward charging; electric trucks are still 2–3% of sales</td></tr>
<tr><td>Williamson (1985): asset specificity</td><td>Assets tied to one partner invite hold-up and favour integration. Test: who owns the swap station and pack?</td><td>The pack is specific to one format</td><td>Closed loops, where the fleet owns both, avoid the hold-up an open network faces</td></tr>
<tr><td>Better Place (2013): single-format precedent</td><td>A swap network tied to one vehicle model failed <span class="ref">[C30]</span></td><td>Passenger cars, not trucks</td><td>Warns against open networks before a standard</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dixit &amp; Pindyck (1994): real options</td><td>Under irreversibility and uncertainty, waiting has value</td><td>Investment is hard to reverse</td><td>Supports waiting on open networks and piloting closed loops</td></tr>
</table>
